1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:06,590
One fall, my wife Elli and I had a single goal: to photograph polar bears.
一年秋天，我和妻子埃莉只有一个目标：拍北极熊。

2
00:00:07,390 --> 00:00:14,570
We were staying at a research camp outside “the polar bear capital of the world”—the town of Churchill in Manitoba, Canada.
我们住在“世界北极熊之都”——加拿大马尼托巴省的丘吉尔镇——外面的一个研究营地里。

3
00:00:15,370 --> 00:00:19,250
Taking pictures of polar bears is amazing but also dangerous.
拍北极熊很精彩，却也很危险。

4
00:00:20,050 --> 00:00:24,950
Polar bears—like all wild animals—should be photographed from a safe distance.
北极熊和所有野生动物一样，应该在安全距离之外拍摄。

5
00:00:25,750 --> 00:00:30,990
When I’m face to face with a polar bear, I like it to be through a camera with a telephoto lens.
和北极熊面对面时，我希望中间隔着一台装了长焦镜头的相机。

6
00:00:31,790 --> 00:00:34,530
But sometimes, that is easier said than done.
可有时候，说起来容易做起来难。

7
00:00:35,330 --> 00:00:36,860
This was one of those times.
这次就是其中之一。

8
00:00:37,660 --> 00:00:44,530
As Elli and I cooked dinner, a young male polar bear who was playing in a nearby lake sniffed and smelled our garlic bread.
我和埃莉做晚饭时，一头在附近湖里玩耍的年轻雄性北极熊嗅了嗅，闻到了我们的大蒜面包。

9
00:00:45,330 --> 00:00:50,250
The hungry bear followed his nose to our camp, which was surrounded by a high wire fence.
饥饿的熊循着气味来到了我们的营地，营地四周围着高高的铁丝网。

10
00:00:51,050 --> 00:00:52,510
He pulled and bit the wire.
它又扯又咬铁丝网。

11
00:00:53,310 --> 00:00:56,520
He stood on his back legs and pushed at the wooden fence posts.
它用后腿站立，推着围栏的木桩。

12
00:00:57,320 --> 00:01:00,860
Terrified, Elli and I tried all the bear defense actions we knew.
我和埃莉吓坏了，把知道的防熊招数全都试了一遍。

13
00:01:01,660 --> 00:01:06,320
We yelled at the bear, hit pots hard, and fired blank shotgun shells into the air.
我们冲着熊大喊，使劲敲锅，还朝天空发射霰弹枪的空包弹。

14
00:01:07,120 --> 00:01:10,170
Sometimes loud noises like these will scare bears off.
有时候，这样的巨响会把熊吓跑。

15
00:01:10,970 --> 00:01:15,730
Not this polar bear though—he just kept trying to tear down the fence with his massive paws.
可这头北极熊不吃这一套——它只是用巨大的爪子不停地想把围栏扯倒。

16
00:01:16,530 --> 00:01:18,500
I radioed the camp manager for help.
我用无线电向营地负责人求救。

17
00:01:19,300 --> 00:01:24,170
He told me a helicopter was on its way, but it would be 30 minutes before it arrived.
他告诉我，直升机已经在路上了，不过还要三十分钟才能到。

18
00:01:24,970 --> 00:01:28,810
Making the best of this close encounter, I took some pictures of the bear.
趁着这次近距离相遇，我给熊拍了几张照片。

19
00:01:29,610 --> 00:01:34,320
Elli and I feared the fence wouldn’t last through 30 more minutes of the bear’s punishment.
我和埃莉担心，围栏再经受这头熊三十分钟的折腾，就撑不住了。

20
00:01:35,120 --> 00:01:38,040
The camp manager suggested I use pepper spray.
营地负责人建议我用胡椒喷雾。

21
00:01:38,840 --> 00:01:41,890
The spray burns the bears’ eyes, but doesn’t hurt them.
这种喷雾会让熊的眼睛火辣辣地疼，但不会伤到它们。

22
00:01:42,690 --> 00:01:47,900
So I approached our uninvited guest slowly and, through the fence, sprayed him in the face.
于是我慢慢走近这位不速之客，隔着围栏朝它脸上喷了一下。

23
00:01:48,700 --> 00:01:52,270
With an angry roar, the bear ran to the lake to wash his eyes.
熊愤怒地吼叫一声，跑到湖边去洗眼睛。

24
00:01:53,070 --> 00:01:56,170
A few minutes later, the bear headed back to our camp.
几分钟后，那头熊又朝我们的营地走了回来。

25
00:01:56,970 --> 00:01:59,900
At that very moment, the helicopter arrived.
就在那一刻，直升机到了。
